    // web-security/cors
    'Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS)' => 'Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS)',
    'In this section, we will explain what cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) is, describe some common examples of cross-origin resource sharing based attacks, and discuss how to protect against these attacks. This topic was written in collaboration with SecDevLab Research, who popularized this attack class with the presentation [[A0]]Exploiting CORS misconfigurations for Bitcoins and bounties[[/A0]].' => 'Bagian ini menjelaskan cara kerja cross-origin resource sharing (CORS), contoh miskonfigurasi yang sering dieksploitasi, serta langkah pencegahannya (merujuk pula riset [[A0]]eksploitasi miskonfigurasi CORS[[/A0]]).',
    'What is CORS (cross-origin resource sharing)?' => 'Apa itu CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)?',
    'Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) is a browser mechanism which enables controlled access to resources located outside of a given domain. It extends and adds flexibility to the same-origin policy (SOP). However, it also provides potential for cross-domain attacks, if a website\'s CORS policy is poorly configured and implemented. CORS is not a protection against cross-origin attacks such as cross-site request forgery (CSRF).' => 'Cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) adalah mekanisme browser yang mengizinkan akses terkontrol terhadap sumber daya di luar origin asal situs, memperluas fleksibilitas same-origin policy (SOP). Namun jika salah dikonfigurasi, CORS justru membuka peluang pencurian data lintas domain. CORS bukan merupakan pelindung terhadap serangan CSRF.',
    'If you\'re already familiar with the basic concepts behind CORS vulnerabilities and just want to practice exploiting them on some realistic, deliberately vulnerable targets, you can access all of the labs in this topic from the link below.' => 'Jika Anda sudah memahami konsep dasar kerentanan CORS dan ingin langsung mempraktikkannya pada target latihan, akses semua lab CORS dari tautan berikut.',
    'The same-origin policy is a restrictive cross-origin specification that limits the ability for a website to interact with resources outside of the source domain. The same-origin policy was defined many years ago in response to potentially malicious cross-domain interactions, such as one website stealing private data from another. It generally allows a domain to issue requests to other domains, but not to access the responses.' => 'Same-origin policy (SOP) membatasi skrip pada satu origin agar tidak bebas membaca data dari origin lain. Umumnya situs masih boleh mengirim permintaan lintas domain, namun browser menahan akses skrip terhadap isi responsnya.',
    'The same-origin policy is very restrictive and consequently various approaches have been devised to circumvent the constraints. Many websites interact with subdomains or third-party sites in a way that requires full cross-origin access. A controlled relaxation of the same-origin policy is possible using cross-origin resource sharing (CORS).' => 'Karena banyak aplikasi modern perlu berbagi API antar subdomain atau mitra pihak ketiga, CORS menyediakan pelonggaran SOP secara terkontrol.',
    'The cross-origin resource sharing protocol uses a suite of HTTP headers that define trusted web origins and associated properties such as whether authenticated access is permitted. These are combined in a header exchange between a browser and the cross-origin web site that it is trying to access.' => 'Protokol CORS menggunakan serangkaian header HTTP untuk menentukan origin yang dipercaya serta apakah kredensial (seperti cookie) diizinkan ikut dikirim saat mengakses sumber daya lintas origin.',
    'CORS and the Access-Control-Allow-Origin response header' => 'CORS dan header respons Access-Control-Allow-Origin',
    'Vulnerabilities arising from CORS configuration issues' => 'Kerentanan akibat kesalahan konfigurasi CORS',
    'Many modern websites use CORS to allow access from subdomains and trusted third parties. Their implementation of CORS may contain mistakes or be overly lenient to ensure that everything works, and this can result in exploitable vulnerabilities.' => 'Kesalahan implementasi daftar izin origin atau kebijakan yang terlalu longgar demi kelancaran integrasi kerap membuka celah pencurian data lintas situs.',
    'Server-generated ACAO header from client-specified Origin header' => 'Header ACAO yang dibuat server dari header Origin klien',
    'Some applications need to provide access to a number of other domains. Maintaining a list of allowed domains requires ongoing effort, and any mistakes risk breaking functionality. So some applications take the easy route of effectively allowing access from any other domain.' => 'Demi menghindari kerumitan mengelola daftar domain, sebagian server mengambil jalan pintas dengan mengizinkan akses dari origin mana pun.',
    'One way to do this is by reading the Origin header from requests and including a response header stating that the requesting origin is allowed. For example, consider an application that receives the following request:' => 'Pola yang kerap ditemui adalah menyalin langsung nilai header Origin dari permintaan klien ke respons header ACAO. Misalnya saat menerima permintaan:',
    'It then responds with:' => 'Server langsung membalas dengan:',
    'These headers state that access is allowed from the requesting domain ([[C0]]) and that the cross-origin requests can include cookies ([[C1]]) and so will be processed in-session.' => 'Respons tersebut menyatakan bahwa domain [[C0]] diizinkan membaca respons sekaligus menyertakan cookie sesi pengguna ([[C1]]).',
    'Because the application reflects arbitrary origins in the [[C0]] header, this means that absolutely any domain can access resources from the vulnerable domain. If the response contains any sensitive information such as an API key or CSRF token, you could retrieve this by placing the following script on your website:' => 'Karena server memantulkan origin sembarang pada header [[C0]], situs berbahaya mana pun dapat menyuruh browser korban mengambil data sensitif seperti API key lewat skrip seperti ini:',
    'Some applications that support access from multiple origins do so by using a whitelist of allowed origins. When a CORS request is received, the supplied origin is compared to the whitelist. If the origin appears on the whitelist then it is reflected in the [[C0]] header so that access is granted. For example, the application receives a normal request like:' => 'Sebagian aplikasi menggunakan whitelist origin lalu memantulkan origin ke [[C0]] apabila lolos pencocokan:',
    'The application checks the supplied origin against its list of allowed origins and, if it is on the list, reflects the origin as follows:' => 'Bila lolos pengecekan daftar izin, server mengirimkan kembali origin tersebut:',
    'Mistakes often arise when implementing CORS origin whitelists. Some organizations decide to allow access from all their subdomains (including future subdomains not yet in existence). And some applications allow access from various other organizations\' domains including their subdomains. These rules are often implemented by matching URL prefixes or suffixes, or using regular expressions. Any mistakes in the implementation can lead to access being granted to unintended external domains.' => 'Kesalahan logika sering terjadi saat mencocokkan prefiks, sufiks, atau ekspresi regex pada whitelist domain sehingga domain milik penyerang ikut lolos.',
